//? Title: Shell sort: actual gapped insertions
//? Subtitle: Actual canonical histories, retained original fixtures and explicit controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
An integer array must be placed in order while avoiding the many short moves of
ordinary insertion sorting. Halving-gap Shell sort first orders interleaved
subsequences, then finishes with gap one. We expose actual pass states, retain
the original seven keys and forty seeded arrays, and compare executed key
comparisons with ordinary insertion.
)

\toc

> Mechanism and mathematical scope

For gap \(h\), insert each key into its already sorted residue-class prefix.
This maintains sorted prefixes inside every interleaved subsequence. The final
\(h=1\) pass is ordinary insertion sort, so its completed prefix invariant
proves the complete result is sorted.

Only comparisons between keys count: a final failed value comparison counts, but
an index-boundary test does not. A displaced-key assignment is a shift; final
placement of the held key is excluded. Passing the boundary performs no extra
value comparison.

$$h_0=\lfloor n/2\rfloor,\qquad h_{k+1}=\lfloor h_k/2\rfloor.$$
\alttext(The original gap sequence repeatedly halves the integer gap until one.)

This particular sequence has quadratic worst-case work. The early passes often
help, but the old report's claim that it necessarily breaks the quadratic
barrier was too strong. It is unstable because long-range shifts can move equal
keys past one another. Without captured histories, owned working storage is
linear; complete pass copies add order n log n values.

> A tiny actual producing history

The table and native concept diagram read the same producing execution. Every
block below is a complete editable C++ program.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/shell_sort_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <filesystem>
#include <print>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cmath>
#include <limits>
#include <numeric>
#include <numbers>
#include <random>
#include <string>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <set>
#include <array>
#include <tuple>
#include <bit>
#include <datamunge/random/quality.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/shell_sort.hpp>
int main() {
    namespace a = dm::algorithms;
    namespace g = dm::graphics;
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    auto r = a::shell_sort_trace({3, 1, 2});
    std::print("| gap | comparisons | shifts | actual state |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (const auto &s : r.passes)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | `{}` |\n", s.gap, s.comparisons, s.shifts,
                   s.after);
    g::Diagram concept_diagram(48, 1100);
    concept_diagram.node("n0", {0, 0}, "Keys 3, 1, 2");
    concept_diagram.node("n1", {5, 0},
                         std::format("Gap {}: {} shifts", r.passes.front().gap,
                                     r.shifts));
    concept_diagram.node("n2", {10, 0}, std::format("Sorted {}", r.values));
    concept_diagram.edge("n0", "n1").edge("n1", "n2");
    concept_diagram.save_svg("build/shell_sort_tiny.svg");
}
```
