> API and interpretation

`shell_sort_run` and `shell_sort_trace` use the same insertion producer. A
failed policy throws; no partial sorted result is returned. Forty arrays use one
mt19937 seed3, sequentially shuffled at sizes10 through400 in steps of10. This
controlled workload is not an asymptotic proof or a timing benchmark.

Reusable producers, fixtures and histories live in the canonical C++ library.
Captured records own their values and do not come from a separate report replay.
Selected counters and record/scalar policies are specified at their API
boundaries; they are not peak-byte or wall-time budgets. Synthetic structural
inputs are controlled fixtures for these mechanisms.

> References

- [Shell (1959), A High-Speed Sorting Procedure|https://doi.org/10.1145/368370.368387]

